text:
jess: Heads up — noticed the sample app's Retrofit client triggers duplicate token refreshes under load, which causes a flurry of 401s and then the UI shows an "Unable to authenticate" dialog. Repro: open two chat windows quickly and trigger the initial streaming request from both.

tom: Which auth pattern are we using? OkHttp Authenticator or an Interceptor?

jess: Authenticator (OkHttp Authenticator) for automatic refresh on 401. The refresh itself uses a blocking Retrofit call inside the authenticator and writes new tokens to SharedPreferences.

nina: Ah. Authenticators run on multiple threads and can race — if two requests hit 401 before the first refresh completes you'll end up issuing two refresh calls. That can invalidate tokens or cause server-side rate-limits.

jess: Example simplified snippet that caused the problem:

```kotlin
class TokenAuthenticator(private val authApi: AuthApi): Authenticator {
  override fun authenticate(route: Route?, response: Response): Request? {
    // synchronous refresh call
    val refreshResp = authApi.refreshTokenSync() // Retrofit.execute() under the hood
    if (!refreshResp.isSuccessful) return null
    val newToken = refreshResp.body()?.accessToken ?: return null
    prefs.set("token", newToken)
    return response.request.newBuilder()
      .header("Authorization", "Bearer $newToken")
      .build()
  }
}
```

jess: On a busy device this caused 2-3 refresh calls concurrently, and sometimes the auth endpoint returned 400 because the old refresh token got rotated.

tom: Fix is to single-flight the refresh so only one network call happens. We solved this before with a Mutex + cached deferred or an AtomicReference of an in-flight Call. Something like: check in-flight token refresh, if present await it, otherwise start refresh and publish result.

nina: Another option is to move refresh into an Interceptor that serializes via synchronized block, but careful: interceptors run on the calling thread and blocking there can increase latency. Authenticator is the right place but needs a single-flight guard.

jess: I put together a small helper used in the SDK samples that uses a Mutex and a CompletableDeferred:

```kotlin
private val refreshMutex = Mutex()
private var refreshDeferred: CompletableDeferred<String>? = null

suspend fun singleFlightRefresh(): String {
  refreshMutex.withLock {
    refreshDeferred?.let { return it.await() }
    val d = CompletableDeferred<String>()
    refreshDeferred = d
    try {
      val resp = authApi.refreshToken() // suspend Retrofit call
      val token = resp.accessToken
      prefs.set("token", token)
      d.complete(token)
      return token
    } catch (e: Exception) {
      d.completeExceptionally(e)
      throw e
    } finally {
      refreshDeferred = null
    }
  }
}
```

jess: Then the Authenticator delegates to this suspend helper via runBlocking to maintain the Authenticator contract. Works reliably in the sample.

android-eng: Two notes: 1) runBlocking in Authenticator is unfortunate but pragmatic; we should avoid long blocking on the OkHttp dispatcher. Consider scheduling the refresh onto a dedicated single-thread executor to avoid starving OkHttp. 2) On Android 13+ we saw some flaky behavior when the app's process backgrounding combined with multiple simultaneous requests — make sure the sample uses process-lifetime token store (not ephemeral in-memory) for correctness.

tom: Also adding tests: simulate concurrent 401 responses in unit test by mocking AuthApi and ensure only one refresh call is invoked. I'll add one to sample-app.

jess: Draft PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sample-app/pull/129 (adds single-flight refresh helper, test, and a short onboarding note).

devex-bot: ci/samples: flaky-emulator detected in matrix, but unit tests passed.

nina: For onboarding docs let's add: "If you see multiple refresh calls / 401 storms: ensure your token refresh is single-flight (mutex/atomic deferred), persist tokens across process restarts, and avoid issuing blocking network calls on OkHttp dispatcher without isolating the executor." I'll author the blurb.

jess: Perfect — thanks all. Leaving the PR as draft until we get the CI matrix fixed.
